  <data name="AttackFactors" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>p * q = n: {0}. The key is broken.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="AttackFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The attack failed for this run.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="AttackP" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>From s he computes p = {0} and q = n / p = {1}.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="AttackRead" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The attacker reads the upper bits of n: {0} (this is c).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="AttackSeed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>He decrypts them with d: s = {0}.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="AttackerKey" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Public key of the attacker, built into the generator: N = {0}, e = {1}. The secret exponent d stays with the attacker.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Conclusion" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Without the secret exponent d, n looks random and is as hard to factor as any other modulus. So the backdoor cannot be found by looking at the keys, only by examining the generator itself. This is a demonstration of one method (the seed is hidden in the upper bits of n); real SETUP attacks use elliptic curves to hide more bits.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Intro" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A changed key generator (a smart card, a library) makes RSA keys that look normal. Without it nobody sees the difference, but the attacker, who knows a secret key, can factor every public key it makes. Small numbers are used here.</value>
  </data>
